@startuml process_termination

title Process Termination

box "Main loop thread" #F0F4FA
participant "ProcessGroupManager" as PGM
participant "Graph" as Graph
participant "Transition" as Transition
end box

queue "Component Event Queue" as EventQueue

box "OsHandler thread" #F4F0FA
participant "OsHandler" as OsHandler
participant "SafeProcessMap" as Map
participant "IComponentController" as Monitor
participant "IComponent" as Component
end box

participant "Operating System" as OS

note over Graph: graph is in state kSuccess:\nthe Run Target is active

' --- detection ---------------------------------------------------------------
OS -> OsHandler ++: a child process has terminated
note right of OsHandler
  The OsHandler thread waits for any child of the Launch
  Manager. It only learns the PID and an exit status,
  not which component the process belongs to.
end note

OsHandler -> Map ++: look up the component for the PID
Map -> Monitor ++: the process has terminated
note right of Map
  The lookup removes the entry that was added when the
  component registered its process - see Component Activation.
end note
Monitor -> Component ++: notify the component of the termination

alt #Pink the exit is not an expected completion
    Component --> Monitor: unexpected termination
    Monitor -> EventQueue: component terminated unexpectedly
    note right of Monitor
      Of a component that has finished starting, only a self
      terminating one that exits with status 0 is a completion.
      Every other exit is an unexpected termination.
    end note

else #White the component is self terminating and exited with status 0
    alt #F2FAF2 an activation is waiting for exactly this termination
        Component --> Monitor: nothing to report
        note right of Monitor
          The ready condition of the component is its own
          termination: the exit releases the waiting worker, and
          that worker reports the successful activation - see
          Component Activation.
        end note

    else #FFF8E8 the component became ready before it exited
        Component --> Monitor: nothing to report
        note right of Monitor
          The expected exit of an already ready component is not
          an event: the graph keeps the component as activated.
        end note
    end

else #FFF8E8 the termination was requested or the component is still starting
    Component --> Monitor: nothing to report
    note right of Monitor
      The worker that is waiting inside the activation or the
      deactivation is released instead, and that worker reports
      the outcome - see Component Deactivation.
    end note
end

deactivate Component
deactivate Monitor
deactivate Map
deactivate OsHandler

' --- reaction ----------------------------------------------------------------
EventQueue -> PGM ++: component event
PGM -> Graph ++: apply the component event
note over Graph
  An unexpected termination always leaves the active Run Target.
end note

Graph -> Component ++: clean up the terminated component
note over Graph
  The clean up is done in place on the main loop thread, not on
  a worker, so that supervision and process resources of the
  terminated component are released immediately.
end note
Component --> Graph --: clean up done

alt #White the Run Target was active (kSuccess)
    note over Graph: the graph moves to kUndefinedState right away
else #White a transition was running (kInTransition)
    note over Graph
      The graph moves to kAborting and stays there until the last
      component in flight has reported back, then it moves to
      kUndefinedState.
    end note
end
deactivate Graph

' --- recovery ----------------------------------------------------------------
PGM -> PGM: handle the graph state
note over PGM
  In kUndefinedState and without a pending request from outside,
  the main loop starts a transition to the fallback Run Target.
end note

PGM -> Graph ++: start a transition to the fallback Run Target
Graph -> Transition ++: set up the transition
Transition --> Graph --: components ready to be stopped
note over Graph
  From here the recovery runs as an ordinary transition
  - see Component Deactivation and Component Activation.
end note

deactivate Graph
deactivate PGM

legend bottom
  The whole notification path runs on the OsHandler thread; the graph only ever
  reacts to the resulting event on the main loop thread.

  A crash of a component that is currently being started or stopped is not an
  unexpected termination: it is part of the outcome of that activation or
  deactivation.
end legend

@enduml
